---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildFAQSchema } from "../../lib/seo";

const pageFaqs = [
  {
    question: "What is the main goal in a problem solution essay?",
    answer:
      "The main goal is to explain the problem clearly and give realistic solutions that actually match the causes or effects you describe.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need to explain the causes before the solutions?",
    answer:
      "Not always, but many students write better essays when they explain the problem first and then give solutions that directly connect to it.",
  },
  {
    question: "What kind of solutions sound weak in IELTS writing?",
    answer:
      "Very broad solutions like 'the government should do more' sound weak unless you explain what should be done and how it would help.",
  },
];

const pageStructuredData = [
  buildBreadcrumbSchema(Astro.site, [
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", path: "/blog/" },
    { name: "IELTS Writing Task 2 Problem Solution Essay", path: "/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-problem-solution/" },
  ]),
  buildFAQSchema(pageFaqs),
];
---

<Layout
  title="IELTS Writing Task 2 Problem Solution Essay | Clear Structure and Realistic Ideas"
  description="A practical IELTS problem solution essay guide with clear paragraphing, stronger causes, and realistic solutions that support a higher score."
  type="article"
  keywords="IELTS problem solution essay, IELTS Task 2 problem solution"
  structuredData={pageStructuredData}
>
  <article class="section guide-article">
    <a class="guide-back-link" href="/blog">Back to blog</a>

    <header class="guide-header">
      <span class="guide-tag">IELTS writing guide</span>
      <h1 style="margin: 0;">IELTS Writing Task 2 problem solution essay</h1>
      <div class="guide-meta">
        <span>March 17, 2026</span>
        <span>7 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="guide-lead">
      <p>
        Problem solution essays often sound weak because the problem is described in a very
        general way and the solution is even more general. A high-scoring response needs a
        clear cause, a clear effect, and a realistic answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        In other words, your solution should feel connected to the problem, not added at
        the end just because the question asked for one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-grid guide-grid-2">
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>What makes this essay type strong</h2>
        <ul class="guide-list">
          <li>The problem is specific, not vague.</li>
          <li>The cause or effect is explained clearly.</li>
          <li>The solution is realistic and easy to understand.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>What often goes wrong</h2>
        <ul class="guide-list">
          <li>The writer lists problems without developing them.</li>
          <li>The solution does not match the problem well.</li>
          <li>The ideas sound too broad or unrealistic.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>A simple structure that works</h2>
      <div class="guide-steps">
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Introduction</h3>
            <p>Paraphrase the topic and say that the issue has clear causes or solutions.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Body paragraph 1</h3>
            <p>Explain the main problem or one important cause. Add one useful example.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">3</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Body paragraph 2</h3>
            <p>Present one or two realistic solutions that directly respond to the problem.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">4</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Conclusion</h3>
            <p>Summarize the problem and show why the solution matters.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>What a realistic solution sounds like</h2>
      <div class="guide-example">
        <p><strong>Weak:</strong> The government should solve this problem.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Better:</strong> Local governments could improve public transport by
          adding more low-cost bus routes, which would reduce the number of people who rely
          on private cars for short daily trips.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="guide-note" style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">
        <p>
          The second solution is stronger because it explains who should act, what they
          should do, and how it would help.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
      <table class="guide-mistake-table">
        <tr>
          <th>Mistake</th>
          <th>Better move</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The problem is described in a broad, abstract way.</td>
          <td>Choose one clear problem and explain it properly.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The solution sounds ideal but not realistic.</td>
          <td>Give a practical solution that could actually happen.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The essay gives a solution without showing why the problem exists.</td>
          <td>Connect the solution to a clear cause or effect.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Too many solutions are listed quickly.</td>
          <td>Use fewer ideas and explain them better.</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>Next step</h2>
      <div class="guide-links">
        <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-advantages-disadvantages">
          <strong>Read the advantages and disadvantages guide</strong>
          <span>Compare this structure with a different Task 2 essay type.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/essay-correction">
          <strong>Check your problem solution essay</strong>
          <span>Find out whether your solutions really match the problem you wrote about.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/ielts/writing">
          <strong>Go to the IELTS writing hub</strong>
          <span>Move to another essay type or a lesson that supports your writing.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <div class="guide-faq">
        {pageFaqs.map((item) => (
          <details class="card">
            <summary>{item.question}</summary>
            <p class="muted" style="margin-top: var(--s-2); margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

  <section class="section-sm" style="max-width: 640px; margin: 0 auto; text-align: center;">
    <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid #ddd; margin-bottom: var(--s-5);" />
    <p class="muted">Want a CLB 9 writing system you can actually follow?</p>
    <a class="btn" href="/celpip/writing">Get the system</a>
  </section>
</Layout>
